$ feov-record show report --help
THE REPORT, as red audits it and blue amends it; add --anchor <id> to read just the passage AT one anchor (with its section and line numbers) rather than the whole document. Anchors are shown AS THEY ARE: blue's `edit` refuses an edit that drops one, so a token inside the span you are replacing is yours to carry into --new. TO LOOK ONE UP rather than carry it: `show findings` resolves `<!--fx:F-…-->`, `show evidence` resolves `<!--cite:C-…-->` and `<!--proof:P-…-->`, and `show board` resolves `<!--gap:G-…-->`. --match and --quote select lines by their text WITH THE ANCHORS OUT, so a pattern crosses an anchor and never names one: to read at an anchor, --anchor is the selector. Written by the opening synthesis and blue's `edit`.

--json gives the same lines structured: `lines` is every line you asked for — the whole report, the window under --anchor, the matching lines under --match or --quote — each with its `line` number and the section `heading` it sits under, and `head` is the report head they were read at. Under --match or --quote, `selection` says how many lines matched and of how many. The bare call is the form to READ; this is the form to select from.

THIS IS THE REPORT'S TEXT, NOT THE ASSEMBLED REPORT. Assembly places this text among sections it composes from the record, and none of those is in what this prints: Outcome, Risk matrix, Research areas, Future research directions, Alternatives considered, Bibliography, The board, The debate, How this run was conducted, The verdict's basis, Log (what the run told the operator), Record verification, Claims retired, Superseded bench statements. Blue's `ingest` and `edit` refuse report text that carries one. --assembled reads the set as the run ships it: every section in shipping order, who composes it and from what, and how content reaches it. With --assembled --json each section that ships carries its `text` as the reader gets it, anchors resolved — the form in which a composed section is read or audited: pipe it to `jq` and select on `heading`.

OUTPUT (JSON, with --json — the bare call is the markdown form): {head,lines:[{line,heading,text}]}

OUTPUT WITH --assembled --json: {sections:[{file,heading,line,composer,from,path,ships,own_in_report,authored_headings:[string],text}]}

`heading` IS SPELLED TWO WAYS. In --assembled --json it is the section's bare name — `Research areas`, no `#` marks. In the --json `lines` it is the heading line as the report writes it — `## Research areas`.

(FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RU…) → SHARED §3

Flags:
      --anchor id     read the report AT one anchor id (F-…, C-…, P-… or G-…) rather than whole — you get the LIVE text there, its section heading, and line numbers to quote back. Takes --window and --json beside it and no other selector: --match, --quote and --assembled are each refused with it
      --assembled     read the ASSEMBLED report set rather than the report's text: every section in shipping order, who composes it and how content reaches it — a listing WITHOUT the sections' text, which the --json form carries. With --json, --match and --quote select its sections; without --json they are refused beside it, and --anchor and --window are refused beside it in either form
      --match regex   select only the report lines matching this regex (RE2, case-insensitive) — an alternation is one call where a phrase at a time is several. Every match is returned and the total is stated; nothing is ranked or cut. Refused beside --anchor. Beside --assembled it selects SECTIONS, and only with --json
      --quote text    select only the report lines containing this text LITERALLY (case-insensitive) — the same span the acting verbs take, so filtering by it first tells you whether it is really in the report. Use this rather than --match whenever the text has (), ., *, ? or [] in it, which a regex would read as syntax and silently match something else. Refused beside --anchor. Beside --assembled it selects SECTIONS, and only with --json
      --window int    ONLY with --anchor, and refused without it: how many paragraphs of content either side of the anchor (blank lines are kept, not counted) (default 3)
========
$ feov-record show work --help
**YOU ARE PROBABLY NOT MEANT TO RUN THIS. The list is delivered with your dispatch, and every act you record answers `may I stop` for you afterwards — so reach for this only when neither reached you, or when you want the items that do NOT block you.** EVERYTHING OPEN TO YOU, in one list. `sitting.open` is every work item, each with `blocks` (whether it stops you closing); `sitting.complete` is true exactly when nothing blocking is left.

An item with `blocks: false` is work nobody will refuse you for skipping — a citation nobody verified, a source blue never cited, a proof nobody re-ran, an avenue never revisited, a grade you could move, a motion you could file. IT IS STILL YOUR WORK: `complete: true` with items open means the gates are satisfied, NOT that nothing is left.

`open` holds OPEN gaps only, and each carries WHAT IT TAKES TO ACT ON IT: the grades, class and location, the WHOLE problem (`problem_synopsis` is the first 140 characters, for scanning a long list), the `required_fix` and the `acceptance_check` you will be re-audited against, `minted_by` and `yours_to_close` — only the seat that minted a gap may close or regrade it. YOU SHOULD NOT NEED THE BOARD TO ACT ON YOUR OWN WORK. Each quote gap carries `location_state`: `marked` — `location` is the sentence holding the gap's anchor in the report as it stands, and `passage` its section; `gone` — the gap's anchor is not in the report — blue cut its sentence, or, in a migrated run, its quote never placed; that is not silence — judge the report as it stands, and `location` is the text as minted; `unrendered` — the report does not render, the board's `anomalies` say why, and `location` is the text as minted. A gap about something that is not report text carries none; `edited_since` lists the edits that changed a gap's sentence since you last sat. One with `remanded` was REMANDED by the bench, and `remand_stage` says where a remand ruled at impasse stands. `owed`: the gap is back in the debate for ONE more exchange — the dispatch readies its minting lens and blue on it, owing the research direction the ruling states (`docket_reopens_on`), and an item on each of their lists says so. `spent`: that exchange has begun — if it leaves the gap at impasse, the dispatch dockets it for the bench again, and if it moved the gap, its limits count afresh from the ruling. `at_limit`: remanded at impasse twice, it stays open at its limit. No `remand_stage`: no remand was ruled while the gap was at impasse.

`estopped` IS WHAT YOU MAY NOT RE-RAISE: the gaps the BENCH ruled, each with id, location, class and the `fate` that ended it. New evidence against one is a lineage successor, minted under a new id naming the ruled gap in `supersedes` and saying what the ruling did not account for. YOUR OWN closures are not here and are not a bar: red may reopen what red closed, and the lens's `near-match` shows you those with `closed_by` at the moment you are deciding reopen-or-new.

Fate defect_owed_elsewhere means still broken and NOT yours to fix; repaired_with_regression means a live successor exists. Written by the lens's `mint` and `close` and the bench's `motion docket rule`

(THIS PROJECTION IS ALREADY THE J…) → SHARED §7

OUTPUT (JSON): {sitting:{seat,role,complete,open:[{what,blocks,another_seat_clears}],last_sitting:{kind,pin,head},mint_budget:{open,budget,left,floor,unit,units,mints,per,measured}},open:[{id,severity,likelihood,impact,complexity_cost,class,location_state,location,passage,about_kind,about_ref,backing:[{anchor,kind,outcome,confidence,verified_by}],edited_since:[{epoch,edited_by,old,new}],problem_synopsis,problem,required_fix,acceptance_check,fix_basis,fix_old,fix_new,minted_by,yours_to_close,regrades_owed:[{motion_id,dimension,proposed}],check_kind,awaiting_proof,remanded,docket_reopens_on,remand_stage,found_by:[string],material}],estopped:[{id,location_state,location,about_kind,about_ref,backing:[{anchor,kind,outcome,confidence,verified_by}],class,fate,closed_by,artifact_state}],counts:{open,estopped},counterparty:{role,acts,acts_this_epoch,last_epoch,reading},engaged:{gap_ids:[string],found_closed:[{id,closed_by_seat,fate}]}}

(FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RU…) → SHARED §3

Flags:
      --match regex   → SHARED §12
      --quote text    → SHARED §13

$ manual --seat-id operator --for blue-respond
exit 0
stdout:
feov-record manual — the blue surface, seat blue-respond: 24 commands, each under a header naming it and followed by the help it prints, run live just now. LEAN ON PURPOSE: a block that repeats word for word on several pages is printed ONCE under SHARED below, and each page marks where it came out (`→ SHARED §n`); what every page would say the same way is said once, before SHARED; a group whose page would only list commands that have pages of their own is left out, and so is this command's own page. The surface's own page comes first.

WORDS THIS SURFACE USES — one word for each concept, and it is the same word on every page, prompt and constitution you read:
  - The report is the research prose written for a reader of the subject: one document from a lane's text to what the run assembles at its end, named by its stage only where the stage matters — your part of the report, the report as `show report` serves it, which is the only way a seat reads it, and the copy assembled for the human reader once the run is over.
  - The run directory is the directory that holds one run: its record, its report and the documents assembled beside it.
  - The record is the run's account of every act, held as events in records/record.db, and every read a seat makes is a projection of it.
  - A run is one research question taken from setup to its outcome.
  - An epoch runs from one chair sitting to the next: the chair relays who sits, the parties the record names sit, and the chair sits again.
  - A sitting is one seat's dispatch, from the prompt the engine hands it to the envelope it returns.
  - An exchange is a red sitting followed by a blue sitting on one gap, and k-max counts them.
  - A seat is one agent's place in the run, registered under a seat id whose role decides the surface it is given.
  - A side is red or blue: red audits the report and passes or fails it, blue researches, writes and repairs it.
  - A lens is a red seat that audits the report for one class of defect, and the only seat that files findings and mints gaps.
  - The chair is red's running seat: it relays who sits, rules blue's motions, spot-checks the stale areas before a PASS and records red's verdict.
  - The bench is the seat that adjudicates the contested docket, rules on motions and stamps how the run ended.
  - A lane is one of blue's parallel first passes at the report, written by one researcher under one method.
  - The synthesizer is the blue seat that merges the lane drafts into the report by union and writes the report's authored surfaces.
  - The engine is the workflow script that dispatches the seats the record says are ready and reads the envelopes they return.
  - The operator is the person running the research, and the seat id their own commands run under.
  - The log is the entries a seat files for the operator with the `log` verb — a missing capability, a defect in the tooling, or friction, never a clean sitting, which is derived from having sat and filed nothing — and none of it is debate material.
  - Friction is one type of log entry: something cost the seat a call, a guess or an act — a refusal, a guessed name, a misread shape, a workaround — and it notes what it expected and why, whether or not anything should change; an act no surface offers at all is a request, not friction.
  - A missing capability is an act a seat needed that no surface offers, and it goes in the log, never on the board.
  - A citation is a source attached to a sentence of the report with the cite verb, hashed and dated so it can be checked again.
  - OCR-derived text is a machine's reading of a scanned document's page images: deterministic, and able to misread, so a citation quoting it records the PDF page its quote sits on and is checked against that page's image.
  - An anchor is the invisible token a citation, a proof, a finding or a gap leaves at its sentence in the report.
  - A proof is a recorded computation: a script that was run, with its hash and exit status.
  - A finding anchor is the anchor a lens's finding leaves at the sentence the finding is about.
  - The verdict is the chair's PASS or FAIL on the board.
  - The outcome is how the run ended: VERIFIED, CEILING, HALTED or UNVERIFIED.
  - A finding is a lens's recorded observation of a defect in the report, or of a dispute with a gap already on the board, graded and not yet minted.
  - A gap is a defect in the report that changes a conclusion or a decision a reader makes, minted onto the board by a lens.
  - A gap is material when its class is always material, or when its class goes by grade and its current severity is medium or above, and an open material gap holds the PASS gate.
  - To retire a claim is to take it out of the report with the retire verb, on the record.
  - A disposition is the bench's ruling value on a docketed gap, and it decides whether the gap closes.
  - A grade motion is a motion contesting a gap's grade, filed by any seat but the chair, which rules it.
  - A scorecard is the numbers a seat is measured on, computed from the record: red's for the lenses and the chair, blue's for blue's seats, the bench's for the bench.
  - An avenue is one approach the research could take on the question — proposed by blue with what would be true if it paid off, moved to pursued, concluded, refuted, deferred, declined or abandoned, ruled on by the chair, and reviewed against the report once per epoch.
  - A citation or proof is reopened when an edit changes its sentence and no sitting opened since has checked it; the evidence lens owes the check and is the seat readied for it, a check by any lens from a sitting opened after the edit clears it, and a PASS waits until one does.

LEFT OFF EVERY PAGE BELOW, because each page would say the same: the `Usage:` block wherever it is exactly the command in the page's header followed by `[flags]`, so a page that prints a Usage is saying something more; and the flag `-h, --help`, which every command takes and which prints that command's page, so a page with no `Flags:` section has no flag of its own.

SHARED BY THE COMMANDS BELOW — each block is printed ONCE here. A page carries a marker ending `→ SHARED §n` exactly where the block was: alone on its line the marker stands for the whole block, and at the end of a flag's line it stands for the rest of that flag's description. A block labelled the last block of its pages is not marked on them:
§1 (on 13 pages):
If you need a verb or a flag that is not listed here, it does not exist for you:
do not improvise around it, and do not hand-write the artifact. Record what you
needed and what you would have done with 'log', as a request — a missing
capability is a fact about the tooling, and the log is how it reaches the operator
who can retool you.

§2 (the last block of 23 pages — every page below that carries no marker for it):
Global Flags:
      --json             emit a structured JSON result (and structured errors) instead of human text
      --run string       the run directory — the PreToolUse hook injects it in a real run, so you rarely type it. A value that DISAGREES with the run you were dispatched into is refused
      --schema           print the event-schema epoch this binary writes, and exit
      --seat-id string   your seat id, as the dispatch prompt states it (SEAT_ID). Pass it ONCE, at register, which binds it to you on the record; every later call resolves it, so typing it is optional. It SELECTS this surface (the verbs listed are the ones your seat may run); a value disagreeing with your registration is refused

§3 (on 20 pages):
FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RUNS a backtick inside double quotes before this tool sees
your text, and records whatever the command printed — or nothing — in its place. Pass every
free-text value by capturing it first with a QUOTED heredoc, then give the flag the variable:

  X=$(cat <<'EOF'
  your text — backticks, apostrophes and $ signs are all literal here
  EOF
  )
  … "$X"

§4 (on 2 pages):
The sentence is found in the report as the record holds it, so the report must be frozen into the record first; before that there is nothing to anchor in.

§5 (on 2 pages):
NO NOTE THE TOOL NUMBERS. Assembly writes the report's notes and numbers them: `[^1]`, `[^2]` … for citations and `[^P1]`, `[^P2]` … for proofs. A footnote reference or definition typed into the report's text under a label of that shape — digits, or P and digits — is refused and nothing is recorded. Blue's `cite` places a citation on the sentence its source backs, and blue's `prove` a proof on the sentence it establishes. No note is typed by hand under a number, or under P and a number: a footnote of the report's own — an aside that is neither a source nor a computation — takes a label that is a word. Where such a label is the subject's own text — a character class in a regular expression — it goes in a code span or a fenced block, and is no footnote there.

§6 (on 2 pages):
WHAT MAY BE IN THE REPORT: research prose for a reader of the SUBJECT, and the markers this tool places — nothing else. No provenance or attribution tags, no notes to another seat, no argument about the run, no narration of how the report was made. A fact that LIMITS THE CONCLUSION stays, re-voiced as a limit on the answer rather than a story about the attempt. Operational facts go to `log`, arguments to `closing`, or to `position` where your seat files one.

§7 (on 2 pages):
your own stable handle, any word you choose: a repeat under the same handle returns the first result instead of acting twice. Only cite, edit and prove take a handle: no other verb on this surface has this flag

§8 (on 5 pages):
REQUIRED — your THINKING for this act, not your process — why you graded, closed, ruled or edited as you did; it is the substance the other side answers. The record already holds WHAT you did, in order, so do not narrate the verbs you ran

§9 (on 3 pages):
ONE EVENT, DIFFERENT CONTRACTS: grade (the chair rules), petition (the bench rules), avenue (the chair rules), docket (the bench rules).

§10 (on 3 pages):
Every seat but the one that rules a subject may file on it; exactly one rules, and `rule` appears only on that seat's surface.

§11 (on 4 pages):
an alternation is one call where a phrase at a time is several. Every match is returned and the total is stated; nothing is ranked or cut

§12 (on 4 pages):
the same span the acting verbs take, so filtering by it first tells you whether it is really in the report. Use this rather than --match whenever the text has (), ., *, ? or [] in it, which a regex would read as syntax and silently match something else

§13 (on 4 pages):
THIS PROJECTION IS ALREADY THE JSON: --json is accepted and, on success, byte-for-byte the same. On an ERROR it prints a JSON envelope on stdout — one object whose keys are verb, role, ok, code, error, with `ok` false — so a pipeline must check `ok` before reading keys.

§14 (on 2 pages):
select only the gaps matching this regex (RE2, case-insensitive) — an alternation is one call where a phrase at a time is several. Every match is returned and the total is stated; nothing is ranked or cut

§15 (on 2 pages):
select only the gaps containing this text LITERALLY (case-insensitive) — the same span the acting verbs take, so filtering by it first tells you whether it is really in the report. Use this rather than --match whenever the text has (), ., *, ? or [] in it, which a regex would read as syntax and silently match something else

========
$ feov-record --help
feov-record — blue seats — the report and its edits, manifest rows, directions. No board verbs at all.

(If you need a verb or a flag tha…) → SHARED §1

Usage:
  feov-record [flags]
  feov-record [command]

Available Commands:
  cite         attach a source to a sentence, when a claim needs evidence a reader can follow to the leaf
  closing      your closing argument on one docketed gap, when the bench is about to rule on it
  edit         change the report — the only path into report.md, and how every repair lands
  fetch        cached, hash-verified web read (replaces WebFetch); serves both sides the same bytes
  help         Help about any command
  ingest       freeze the synthesized report into the record and remove the file — done once, by its author
  log          an entry for the operator who can retool you — what it asserts, said in the positive
  manifest-row your receipt for one repaired gap: what you checked, and what checking it showed
  position     blue-respond's position for a sitting — its argument to the bench, the BLUE section; no other blue seat files one
  prove        settle a claim by RUNNING something, when computing the answer beats arguing about it
  register     NOT needed to look, only to RECORD — your first act in a sitting you write to, and the one call needing your seat id
  retire       take a claim out of the report, when it cannot stand and no edit will save it

Command groups — each holds commands THIS page does not list:
  avenue       an approach the research could take, and what became of it — `propose` one, `move` one you already proposed
  motion       file and rule on a motion — grade (the chair rules), petition (the bench rules), avenue (the chair rules), docket (the bench rules). One mechanism, one id.
  show         read a projection of the record — the tool is the read path, and the .md files are for human verification. Bare, it answers with YOUR PENDING WORK

Flags:
  (every entry under that block's heading) → SHARED §2
  -v, --version          version for feov-record

Use "feov-record [command] --help" for more information about a command.
========
$ feov-record avenue move --help
say what became of an avenue you proposed — every open avenue owes this before your sitting ends

An open avenue — proposed or pursued — owes this before your sitting ends; re-recording `pursued` with what you learned is a REAFFIRMATION and settles it for this sitting. An approach you RAN ends at what it found, and owes nothing further: `concluded` where the hypothesis held, `refuted` where what it found contradicted it — a refutation is an answer, and the report prints it beside the confirmations. `abandoned` is only for an approach you started that gave no answer, and `declined` only for one you never ran.

THE REASON IS PRINTED IN THE REPORT, on the avenue's own row beside its question and its hypothesis, in the section the fate you give it here puts it under: as what was found for an approach that ran, and as why it stands there for one that did not. THE TOOL COMPOSES THOSE SECTIONS, WHOLE, from the avenues on the record: Research areas holds each avenue that is proposed, pursued, concluded or refuted, Future research directions each one deferred, and Alternatives considered each one declined or abandoned. They are not the report's text to write: a report that carries one of those headings is refused, and a list of approaches and their fates kept by hand anywhere in it is a second account that nothing checks against the record.

Give the account in the SUBJECT'S terms — what was found about the question that settled this fate — not the steps you took to find it. A reason states the outcome; it does not narrate an earlier version of itself, and a move that corrects one says only what stands. No provenance or attribution tags, no notes to another seat, no narration of the run. "The trial-division bound is 9, so no subtler test is needed" is a reason; "fetched and read the sources at the leaf, then grepped the repo" is a diary. What you did, in order, is already on the record.

(If you need a verb or a flag tha…) → SHARED §1

(FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RU…) → SHARED §3

IF WHAT YOU RECORDED WAS WRONG, say so in a move of the avenue.

Flags:
      --as as-value     REQUIRED — the fate of this avenue
      --id avenue-id    REQUIRED — the avenue whose fate you are moving; the avenues projection lists every one
      --reason string   the account of what settled this fate, in the SUBJECT's terms — printed in the report beside the avenue

Enumerated values:
  --as
    proposed   you intend to follow this line and have not resolved it; the tool assigns it an id, red may rule on it, and it owes a move
    pursued    you are following the line — say what you learned in --reason. It stays open: each sitting re-records it with what changed, or moves it to its end
    concluded  you RAN the approach and the hypothesis HELD — REQUIRES a reason saying what it found, which is in the report; it owes no further move. An approach that ran and refuted its hypothesis is `refuted`, and one that gave no answer is `abandoned`
    refuted    you RAN the approach and what it found CONTRADICTED the hypothesis — REQUIRES a reason saying what it found, which is in the report; it owes no further move. A refutation is an answer, and the row prints beside the concluded ones. An approach that gave no answer is `abandoned`, not this
    deferred   not this run. REQUIRES a reason saying what a later run should pick it up FOR
    declined   you considered it and did NOT run it. REQUIRES a reason. An approach you ran is not this, whatever it found: `concluded` if the hypothesis held, `refuted` if what it found contradicted it, `abandoned` if it was started and no answer came
    abandoned  you STARTED it and NO ANSWER CAME — the method broke, the data was unavailable, or the question was given up. REQUIRES a reason saying what stopped it. An approach that ran and refuted its hypothesis is `refuted`, not this
========
$ feov-record avenue propose --help
put an avenue on the record, when something is worth trying and a later run needs to know you chose it

The tool assigns the id. It starts as `proposed` — the state that OWES a move — so say what became of it with `avenue move`.

You may propose one AT ANY POINT in a run, not only at the start.

WHAT YOU WRITE HERE IS PRINTED IN THE REPORT word for word, on this avenue's row in the section its fate puts it under, each part under its own label: the line as the question the avenue asks, the hypothesis as its hypothesis. THE TOOL COMPOSES THOSE SECTIONS, WHOLE, from the avenues on the record: Research areas holds each avenue that is proposed, pursued, concluded or refuted, Future research directions each one deferred, and Alternatives considered each one declined or abandoned. They are not the report's text to write: a report that carries one of those headings is refused, and a list of approaches and their fates kept by hand anywhere in it is a second account that nothing checks against the record.

THE LINE IS THE QUESTION THE AVENUE ASKS, AND IT HOLDS NO ANSWER. A result you expect is a prediction, and the prediction is the hypothesis: "whether a single Fermat test settles whether 91 is prime" is a line, and "a passing test in one base is no evidence of primality" is its hypothesis. A line that states a result is printed as the question whatever the research then finds, and it stands as proposed: no act rewrites a line.

So write it as research prose for a reader of the SUBJECT — the question the avenue asks, not a log of what you did to ask it. No provenance or attribution tags, no notes to another seat, no narration of the run: "whether 91's factorisation follows from a divisibility test short of trial division" is a line, "ran this lane's slice against the last avenue and grepped the repo" is a diary. Who moved it, the path it took and red's ruling are already on the record, and ship in avenues.md.

(If you need a verb or a flag tha…) → SHARED §1

(FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RU…) → SHARED §3

IF WHAT YOU RECORDED WAS WRONG, say so in a move of the avenue.

Flags:
      --hypothesis string   your PREDICTION: what would be TRUE if this avenue pays off. The report prints it as the avenue's hypothesis, and the fate is judged against it — concluded where it held, refuted where it did not
      --method string       the source class or technique it belonged to, when that is what distinguishes it
      --reason string       REQUIRED — the avenue's LINE: the question it asks, holding no predicted result — research prose for a reader of the SUBJECT, printed in the report word for word as the question
========
$ feov-record cite --help
attach a source to a sentence, when a claim needs evidence a reader can follow to the leaf

The tool fetches and caches the source and splices an invisible anchor at the sentence — you never type a footnote. It survives every later edit: `edit` refuses a replacement that drops one.

(The sentence is found in the rep…) → SHARED §4

To RESOLVE an anchor you meet while reading (not create one), read the `evidence` projection, which lists every source by anchor id.

OCR-DERIVED TEXT CAN MISREAD. A leaf citation of it names the span you quote from the source's reading, verbatim, and the tool records the PDF page that span sits on — never type a page. The span must sit whole on one page of the reading; the refusal says which fix applies when it does not.

THE TITLE IS PRINTED IN THE REPORT, in the source's note and its Bibliography entry; the tool adds the PDF page to the note of a quote from OCR text. Give the source's own name — author, work, section — for a reader of the SUBJECT, not an account of how you found or read it: no "this sitting" or "this run", no "re-read at the leaf". How much of the source you read is recorded as its own field, not in the title's words. Where that reading matters to a claim, say it in the report's prose, in the subject's terms: "the source, read in full, states…", "the abstract claims, and the body does not show, …". The tool flags a title that sounds like the run talking about itself; it refuses nothing, because a real source's own title can.

(If you need a verb or a flag tha…) → SHARED §1

(FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RU…) → SHARED §3

IF WHAT YOU RECORDED WAS WRONG, say so in a new citation.

Flags:
      --key string           your own stable handle, any word you choose: a repeat under the same handle returns the first result instead of acting twice; the TOOL mints the citation's id. Only cite, edit and prove take a handle: no other verb on this surface has this flag
      --ocr-quote string     for OCR-derived text: the span you quote, verbatim from the source's reading (not the report). The tool records the PDF page it sits on; required with --source-text leaf
      --quote string         REQUIRED — the EXACT report text, verbatim and NOTHING else — no section heading, dash or pipe: the whole string is matched against the report, so anything prepended matches nothing. Name the section in --reason, where prose belongs. A mis-quote is rejected rather than guessed at
      --reason string        why this source backs the sentence — kept on the record and shown beside the source in the evidence view, never printed in the report
      --source-text unread   how much of the source you actually READ; omitted records unread. A leaf reading is refused on a copy fetch recorded as the work's abstract, or as not the work
      --title string         REQUIRED — the source's name, as it appears in the composed bibliography
      --url string           REQUIRED — the source's http/https URL — fetched once and cached, so both sides read the same bytes

Enumerated values:
  --source-text
    unread        the text was never read — the citation rests on a record that the source EXISTS (a bibliographic index, a search result), not on anything it says
    summary_only  read only through someone else's account of it — an abstract, a secondary description, or the summary of an INTERESTED party. Everything the report says about its contents is that account, not the source
    leaf          the source's own text was read at the leaf, in the bytes the run cached. The only value that licenses a claim about what the source SAYS
