$ manual --seat-id operator
exit 0
stdout:
feov-record manual — the operator surface, seat operator: 17 commands, each under a header naming it and followed by the help it prints, run live just now. LEAN ON PURPOSE: a block that repeats word for word on several pages is printed ONCE under SHARED below, and each page marks where it came out (`→ SHARED §n`); what every page would say the same way is said once, before SHARED; a group whose page would only list commands that have pages of their own is left out, and so is this command's own page. The surface's own page comes first.

WORDS THIS SURFACE USES — one word for each concept, and it is the same word on every page, prompt and constitution you read:
  - The report is the research prose written for a reader of the subject: one document from a lane's text to what the run assembles at its end, named by its stage only where the stage matters — your part of the report, the report as `show report` serves it, which is the only way a seat reads it, and the copy assembled for the human reader once the run is over.
  - The run directory is the directory that holds one run: its record, its report and the documents assembled beside it.
  - The record is the run's account of every act, held as events in records/record.db, and every read a seat makes is a projection of it.
  - A run is one research question taken from setup to its outcome.
  - An epoch runs from one chair sitting to the next: the chair relays who sits, the parties the record names sit, and the chair sits again.
  - A sitting is one seat's dispatch, from the prompt the engine hands it to the envelope it returns.
  - An exchange is a red sitting followed by a blue sitting on one gap, and k-max counts them.
  - A seat is one agent's place in the run, registered under a seat id whose role decides the surface it is given.
  - A side is red or blue: red audits the report and passes or fails it, blue researches, writes and repairs it.
  - A lens is a red seat that audits the report for one class of defect, and the only seat that files findings and mints gaps.
  - The chair is red's running seat: it relays who sits, rules blue's motions, spot-checks the stale areas before a PASS and records red's verdict.
  - The bench is the seat that adjudicates the contested docket, rules on motions and stamps how the run ended.
  - A lane is one of blue's parallel first passes at the report, written by one researcher under one method.
  - The synthesizer is the blue seat that merges the lane drafts into the report by union and writes the report's authored surfaces.
  - The engine is the workflow script that dispatches the seats the record says are ready and reads the envelopes they return.
  - The operator is the person running the research, and the seat id their own commands run under.
  - The log is the entries a seat files for the operator with the `log` verb — a missing capability, a defect in the tooling, or friction, never a clean sitting, which is derived from having sat and filed nothing — and none of it is debate material.
  - Friction is one type of log entry: something cost the seat a call, a guess or an act — a refusal, a guessed name, a misread shape, a workaround — and it notes what it expected and why, whether or not anything should change; an act no surface offers at all is a request, not friction.
  - A missing capability is an act a seat needed that no surface offers, and it goes in the log, never on the board.
  - A citation is a source attached to a sentence of the report with the cite verb, hashed and dated so it can be checked again.
  - OCR-derived text is a machine's reading of a scanned document's page images: deterministic, and able to misread, so a citation quoting it records the PDF page its quote sits on and is checked against that page's image.
  - An anchor is the invisible token a citation, a proof, a finding or a gap leaves at its sentence in the report.
  - A proof is a recorded computation: a script that was run, with its hash and exit status.
  - A finding anchor is the anchor a lens's finding leaves at the sentence the finding is about.
  - The verdict is the chair's PASS or FAIL on the board.
  - The outcome is how the run ended: VERIFIED, CEILING, HALTED or UNVERIFIED.
  - A finding is a lens's recorded observation of a defect in the report, or of a dispute with a gap already on the board, graded and not yet minted.
  - A gap is a defect in the report that changes a conclusion or a decision a reader makes, minted onto the board by a lens.
  - A gap is material when its class is always material, or when its class goes by grade and its current severity is medium or above, and an open material gap holds the PASS gate.
  - To retire a claim is to take it out of the report with the retire verb, on the record.
  - Lens retirement is the record's state for a lens that has stopped finding material: three sittings in a row without a fresh material gap retire it, and a head move does not ready a retired lens.
  - A stale area is the area of a retired lens whose last sitting is behind the report head, which the chair reads and names in a spot-check before a PASS.
  - A disposition is the bench's ruling value on a docketed gap, and it decides whether the gap closes.
  - A grade motion is a motion contesting a gap's grade, filed by any seat but the chair, which rules it.
  - A scorecard is the numbers a seat is measured on, computed from the record: red's for the lenses and the chair, blue's for blue's seats, the bench's for the bench.
  - An avenue is one approach the research could take on the question — proposed by blue with what would be true if it paid off, moved to pursued, concluded, refuted, deferred, declined or abandoned, ruled on by the chair, and reviewed against the report once per epoch.
  - A citation or proof is reopened when an edit changes its sentence and no sitting opened since has checked it; the evidence lens owes the check and is the seat readied for it, a check by any lens from a sitting opened after the edit clears it, and a PASS waits until one does.

LEFT OFF EVERY PAGE BELOW, because each page would say the same: the `Usage:` block wherever it is exactly the command in the page's header followed by `[flags]`, so a page that prints a Usage is saying something more; and the flag `-h, --help`, which every command takes and which prints that command's page, so a page with no `Flags:` section has no flag of its own.

SHARED BY THE COMMANDS BELOW — each block is printed ONCE here. A page carries a marker ending `→ SHARED §n` exactly where the block was: alone on its line the marker stands for the whole block, and at the end of a flag's line it stands for the rest of that flag's description. A block labelled the last block of its pages is not marked on them:
§1 (the last block of 16 pages — every page below that carries no marker for it):
Global Flags:
      --json             emit a structured JSON result (and structured errors) instead of human text
      --run string       the run directory — the PreToolUse hook injects it in a real run, so you rarely type it. A value that DISAGREES with the run you were dispatched into is refused
      --schema           print the event-schema epoch this binary writes, and exit
      --seat-id string   your seat id, as the dispatch prompt states it (SEAT_ID). Pass it ONCE, at register, which binds it to you on the record; every later call resolves it, so typing it is optional. It SELECTS this surface (the verbs listed are the ones your seat may run); a value disagreeing with your registration is refused

§2 (on 2 pages):
FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RUNS a backtick inside double quotes before this tool sees
your text, and records whatever the command printed — or nothing — in its place. Pass every
free-text value by capturing it first with a QUOTED heredoc, then give the flag the variable:

  X=$(cat <<'EOF'
  your text — backticks, apostrophes and $ signs are all literal here
  EOF
  )
  … "$X"

========
$ feov-record --help
feov-record — the seat-side record runtime (the verb set IS the role boundary)

A lens mints its own gaps and, as the originator, regrades and closes them; no
other seat has a mint, regrade or close verb. Blue has no board verbs at all. The
bench rules and never originates.

Usage:
  feov-record [flags]
  feov-record [command]

Available Commands:
  capture        run /research's post-hoc capture auditor (operator; nine audits + cost.md/tarball/scorecards)
  count-claims   count the FOOTNOTED declarative claims in blue's report (read-only)
  dashboard      render a run's live dashboard.html (operator; board/cost/seats/judiciary/scorecards)
  engine-stopped record that the engine stopped the run on an error (operator; writes the error to the log and NO outcome, so the run stays open to resume)
  fetch          cached, hash-verified web read (replaces WebFetch); serves both sides the same bytes
  graph          render a run's actual behaviour (seat flow + gap lifecycle) as mermaid or dot (read-only)
  help           Help about any command
  manual         render a surface's commands, each with its own help, run live — for generating a seat's constitution
  migrate        replay an old record through this binary's write path into a fresh run (the source is never modified)
  ocr            render a scanned document's pages and read them with the local OCR engine
  ops            ask an operator question about a run — how the seats behaved, what they reported, what answered them (read-only)
  scorecard      print this run's scorecards — red, blue and bench, or one with --card. OPERATOR ANALYTICS ACROSS CARDS; no seat surface carries a scorecard view
  setup          build a research run's blackboard: skeleton, pins, memory mirrors, and the .run-live marker (operator; writes files)
  verify         cross-check a run's record against its invariants, and tally it (read-only)

Flags:
  (every entry under that block's heading) → SHARED §1
  -v, --version          version for feov-record

Use "feov-record [command] --help" for more information about a command.
========
$ feov-record capture --help
capture is the mechanical half of /research's run-record step plus the mechanized post-hoc auditor: it copies the journal, packs the agent transcripts, writes cost.md, runs the nine integrity audits (recomputing counts from the git-tracked files and diffing them against the envelopes' self-reports), appends each card's scorecard, harvests judicial rulings into law/proposed/, and removes the run-live marker — writing run-record-audit.md and exiting 2 on any audit FAIL. The record-backed audits read the record in-process. Ported from capture-research-run.mjs.

Usage:
  feov-record capture <runDir> <transcriptDir> [flags]
========
$ feov-record count-claims --help
count-claims prints the report's claim_count — the number of footnoted declarative claims, computed deterministically from the report on the record. It writes nothing, and it is the count's one definition: no seat relays the number.
========
$ feov-record dashboard --help
dashboard renders the run's own instruments — board-mass trend, open/close rates, live seats + completion ETA, cost-so-far, judiciary analytics, the log, and this run's scorecards — into <runDir>/dashboard.html (meta-refreshes every 20s). Reads the record in-process plus the Workflow transcripts. --watch regenerates every 15s until the .run-live marker is removed. --serve <port> instead HOSTS it over HTTPS behind a per-run secret URL (rendered fresh per request, self-tears-down when the run ends) so it is reachable across the local network. Ported from render-run-dashboard.mjs.

Usage:
  feov-record dashboard <runDir> <transcriptDir> [flags]

Flags:
      --judgment-model string   judgment-tier model, for the run-config header
      --lanes string            blue lane count, for the run-config header
      --model string            bulk-tier model, for the run-config header (else read from inputs/run-config.json)
      --now int                 inject the clock in unix-ms (default: real time) — for deterministic tests
      --serve int               host the dashboard over HTTPS on this port instead of writing a file — bound to 0.0.0.0 so it is reachable across the local network, gated by a per-run secret URL (self-signed cert, rendered fresh per request, self-tears-down when the run ends)
      --watch                   regenerate every 15s until the run-live marker is removed
========
$ feov-record engine-stopped --help
engine-stopped --reason "<the error>" writes the error the engine stopped on to the run's log, as the tool's own `failure` entry under `harness`.

Run it when the Workflow tool reports that the debate FAILED after seats sat. Give the error verbatim: it is the only account of why the run stopped, and with it on the record a run the engine stopped reads differently from one that was cut off.

IT RECORDS NO OUTCOME. An outcome closes the record to every seat; this leaves it open, so the same workflow run resumes against it. The answer says which the record holds: no outcome (resumable), or the outcome a sitting recorded before the stop (finished).

`ops log` reads the entry back, beside what the seats filed.

(FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RU…) → SHARED §2

Flags:
      --reason string   the error the engine stopped on, verbatim
========
$ feov-record fetch --help
fetch GETs --url once, caches the bytes at <run>/cache/<sha256>, and prints A SUMMARY NAMING THE FILES — never the document itself. Open what you need with Read, using an offset and a limit. A PDF's text is extracted to <run>/cache/<sha256>.txt and named in the summary, so you need no PDF tooling.

A PDF WITH NO TEXT LAYER (a scan) is read by the LOCAL OCR ENGINE compiled into this binary and named in the summary as ocr_derived text. A ruled table is reconstructed into |-separated rows; one the engine cannot reconstruct falls back to plain text WITH the reason stated. A document over the render disk budget is refused rather than partly read. OCR text can misread: citing it at the leaf takes blue's `cite --ocr-quote` with the span from the reading, and the tool records the PDF page it sits on.

A later fetch of the same URL is served from cache, so every seat reads identical bytes. It writes no record event. A failure is a non-zero error (pick another source), and fetch writes no log entry for it.

AN UNREACHED SOURCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE: behind an egress proxy, a host outside the allowlist answers 403 — the status an origin uses to refuse a client — so a failure can be a fact about THIS CONTAINER or about the SOURCE, and those are different findings. The refusal says which where it can; where you cannot tell, record the source as UNREACHABLE FROM HERE, not the question as unresolved.

Flags:
      --at string     with --via archive: bound the capture to YYYYMMDD and take the latest at or before it. A priority question needs the FIRST time something was visible, which the newest capture cannot answer
      --ocr           read a PDF that has no text layer with the local OCR engine; --ocr=false caches it unread (default true)
      --url string    REQUIRED — the http/https URL to read
      --via backend   reach the source through a named backend: live | archive | oa | metadata | arxiv | eric | auto. They answer DIFFERENT questions — live the URL itself (the same as omitting this); archive what the page said on a date (right for web pages, usually the landing page for a subscription article); oa whether a legal open copy exists; metadata only that the source exists and where (no text, and the honest answer when there is none to get); arxiv the preprint, in whichever form this tool can take — its PDF, or arXiv's own HTML rendering where the PDF is over the fetch cap, which HAS NO PAGE NUMBERS and must be quoted as the HTML; eric the US education index's record, with its full text where ERIC holds an authorised copy; auto tries arxiv, oa, archive, metadata in that order. A refused live fetch falls back through that same order
========
$ feov-record graph --help
graph replays <run>'s record and prints its shape — the seats that ran per epoch (chair sitting) with their event tallies, and each gap's lifecycle coloured by state, with holes (a dispute nobody answered, a silent closure) highlighted. Default output is markdown with mermaid (renders in an artifact, a PR, or a phone); --format dot emits a graphviz digraph for a dense run.

Flags:
      --format string   output format: mermaid (markdown, renders natively) | dot (graphviz) (default "mermaid")
========
$ feov-record migrate --help
migrate reads an old run's record — including a vocabulary this binary's readers refuse — translates each event through the authored registry, and replays them in order through the current write path under the ORIGINAL clock. Every act is validated exactly as a seat's would be, so the output is a record this binary could have written; run channels (inputs, proofs) copy verbatim, and inputs/migration.json records what was translated, refused, and accepted as loss. A refusal exits non-zero and is the tool's OUTPUT — there is no flag that skips validation.

THE PER-TURN MEASUREMENTS ARE BROUGHT ACROSS, not replayed: they are measurements taken from outside, from transcripts that are not part of a run, so the record holds the only copy of them and the manifest states how many came over. The manifest also states the epoch the source declared beside the one this binary wrote, so the move the migration exists to make is a fact you can read rather than infer.

THE REPORT'S TEXT IS BROUGHT INTO THE ORDER IT SHIPS IN (the title line (`# …`); `## TL;DR`; `## The Catechism`; the report's own sections, in the order their author chooses; `## Open questions`), where that moves whole sections and nothing else: the base is reordered and every act replayed over it, and the result is kept when no act is refused and the acts give the same text section for section. Otherwise the record is replayed as written and its text keeps its author's order. The manifest's report_order says which: no_report, in_order, reordered, as_written.

Usage:
  feov-record migrate --from <oldRunDir> --to <newRunDir> [flags]

Flags:
      --accept-loss strings   accept a registry-documented loss for exactly this word (repeatable); the acceptance lands in the manifest
      --from string           the old run directory (its records/record.db file set is read from a copy)
      --to string             the fresh run directory to write — refused if it already holds anything
========
$ feov-record ocr --help
ocr operates on a document `fetch` has already cached. `ocr pages` rasterises the document and names the images; `ocr read` runs the local OCR engine over them and records the reading. A document whose text layer was already extracted is refused unless --force, because reading pixels re-derives, less accurately, what a file already says.

Usage:
  feov-record ocr [flags]
  feov-record ocr [command]

Available Commands:
  pages       rasterise every page of a cached PDF and name the images
  read        read the rendered pages with the local OCR engine, and record the reading

(Global Flags:) → SHARED §1

Use "feov-record ocr [command] --help" for more information about a command.
========
$ feov-record ocr pages --help
pages renders each page of the cached document --sha to a GRAYSCALE PNG under <run>/cache/<sha>.pages/ and writes a render record beside them holding the resolution, one hash per page image, and the renderer's library@semver. --dpi renders EVERY page at one resolution, 300 by default. The automatic scan-reading path does not: it follows each page's own scan resolution, floored at 300 — where the detector's constants were measured — and capped at 600, so a document of mixed scans is a range rather than one number. `ocr read` accepts any page inside that 300-600 band and refuses one outside it. Rendering the same scan here at 300 and letting the automatic path render it at its own higher resolution produces two different readings of one document; --dpi is for a human inspecting the pixels, and the reading an audit re-derives is the automatic one. It prints a summary naming the directory and the page range — never an image, and never the document. A DOCUMENT THAT ALREADY HAS TEXT IS REFUSED. Where fetch extracted a text layer, its extraction is already on disk and reading the pixels instead re-derives it, less accurately. --force renders anyway, for the case where the text layer is present but wrong.

Flags:
      --dpi int      render resolution, 72–600 (default 300)
      --force        render even though a text layer was already extracted
      --sha string   REQUIRED — sha256 of a document already in the run cache (from a fetch summary)
========
$ feov-record ocr read --help
read runs the OCR engine compiled into this binary (tesseract + leptonica, statically linked) over each page image rendered by `ocr pages`. The assembled text is written to <run>/cache/<sha>.ocr.txt and each page's own reading beside its image. FULLY LOCAL AND DETERMINISTIC: no model, no credentials, no network. The record keys the reading to the engine identity and the exact image hashes, so an audit can re-derive it byte for byte. It reads any page rendered between 300 and 600 DPI — the band the detector's constants are derived over, one page at a time from that page's own resolution — and refuses a page outside it; re-render if the images are lower. A page with a ruled table is detected and reconstructed into |-separated rows, with confidence stats as fields on the record; a reconstruction that cannot place its marks falls back to plain text WITH the failure stated, never a plausible half-table. Pages already read are never derived twice — each carries a receipt validated against its image hash — and --force discards the receipts for a full re-read.

Flags:
      --force        read again even though a reading of these exact images exists
      --sha string   REQUIRED — sha256 of a document whose pages ocr pages has already rendered
========
$ feov-record ops diagnostics --help
diagnostics answers, for each seat that registered in a run, whether the surface reached it at all.

SEEN is EXPOSURE — the role's own verbs that appeared in help output the seat received — and it is a
different quantity from USE. A seat can reach for a verb it was never shown (it guessed, or a refusal
taught it) and can be shown verbs it never reaches for. Reporting one as the other is how a naming
experiment measures its own instrument.

SEEN is counted per BLOCK, never per line: the root listing carries names that are also role verbs, so
a line-level match credits every seat before it acts.

Flags:
      --format string       json (the form a reader joins on) | markdown (default "json")
      --sitting string      the seat whose sitting this trajectory is (REQUIRED: one trajectory is one sitting, and the record cannot say which of the registered seats it belongs to)
      --trajectory string   path to the captured trajectory JSONL; inferred from the run when omitted
========
$ feov-record ops log --help
ops log prints the entries seats filed in the log — missing capabilities, defects in the tooling and friction — and, separately, the seats that SAT AND FILED NOTHING, read off the harness brackets rather than asserted by the seat. The two counts are not interchangeable: no entries and no seated-and-silent is a run nobody sat in.

Seats WRITE the log with their own `log --type <type> --reason "..."`, under their own --seat-id; this is the read, and it is yours, not theirs.
========
$ feov-record ops tiers --help
tiers joins the run's CONFIGURED model tiers (inputs/run-config.json — the request) against the model that actually answered each seat — read from that seat's own trajectory, which `register` names by recording its agent id, and where the harness declares a substitution by naming both ends).

A seat nobody measured reads NOT MEASURED. It never reads as the configured model, and it never reads as a blank that could be mistaken for agreement: a run where nothing looked is not a run that matched.

Flags:
      --format string   json (the form a reader joins on) | markdown (default "json")
========
$ feov-record ops timing --help
timing answers which turns a run spent its wall clock in, and what those turns were doing.

THE BUCKETS ARE FACTS, NOT JUDGEMENTS. A threshold — what counts as a stall, what counts as healthy
generation — is chosen from a particular run, and freezing one here would apply it to every future run
by readers who never saw it chosen. So the split is by what each turn CONTAINED, and span_ms and
output_tokens come through per turn so an analysis applies its own cutoffs where they can be argued with.

A turn's span is the gap to the turn BEFORE it, so a seat's first turn has none: null, never zero.
Zero would read as instant, and any total summing it under-reports that seat by its opening turn.

A SPAN IS A GAP, AND A GAP HOLDS WHATEVER FELL INSIDE IT. The measurement is the time between two of a
seat's turns, so each span also holds the execution of any tool the PREVIOUS turn called, not only this
turn's own generation. A bucket names what its turns CONTAINED, never what their time was spent on:
turns carrying a thinking block can lead the totals in a run whose clock actually went to tool calls,
because every one of those gaps holds the call that came before it. Say which you mean.

measured says whether the per-turn measurement was TAKEN. False means the transcripts were never
read for this run, so there is nothing to decompose — a different fact from a run whose turns were
all fast, and one an empty answer on its own cannot tell you.
========
$ feov-record scorecard --help
scorecard prints this run's scorecards — red, blue and bench, in that order, each under its own `# <card> scorecard` heading — or only the one --card names. A card's rows are computed from the run's record (the board, findings and debate projections, read in-process), its journal envelopes and its board telemetry: the same numbers the dashboard and the human see. The envelope-derived rows read "not computed" until capture assembles the journal. OPERATOR ANALYTICS ACROSS CARDS: no seat surface carries a scorecard view. A record that cannot be read is refused before any card is printed.

Usage:
  feov-record scorecard --run <dir> [--card red|blue|bench] [flags]

Flags:
      --card string   which card: red|blue|bench (omit to print all three)
========
$ feov-record setup --help
setup creates <runDir>'s blackboard skeleton (idempotent — pre-staged files are kept), pins the evidence base at HEAD, mirrors the law memory into inputs/, writes the .run-live marker hook guards consult, and preflights the record binary. The four fail-fast gates (a runDir, both model tiers, valid cites, and — with --bin-dir — a matching record binary) run BEFORE any state is created, so a bad launch costs a re-run, not a seat mid-sitting.

(FREE TEXT AND THE SHELL. Bash RU…) → SHARED §2

Usage:
  feov-record setup <runDir> [flags]

Flags:
      --allow-substitution           accept a run whose environment answers with a model other than the configured tier — recorded on the run, so every seat's register stops refusing it and the substitution stays visible on the record
      --bin-dir string               where the feov-record binary the SEATS will call lives (default: this executable's own directory); the version preflight always runs and always refuses on a miss
      --cite stringArray             a cited path, optionally pinned: <path>[@<commit>] (repeatable)
      --convergence-fraction float   fraction of the run's peak board mass below which a FAIL over a board with nothing material is refused (default 0.25)
      --judgment-model string        the judgment tier (blue-synthesize, red-chair, judge, assemble)
      --k int                        consecutive exchanges on one gap with no movement before it is at impasse (default 2; recorded in run-config.json)
      --k-max int                    total exchanges on one gap before it is at impasse regardless of movement (default 6; a smoke run passes 2)
      --lanes string                 the frontier lane count (recorded in run-config.json)
      --lens-area stringArray        a red lens area this run dispatches (repeatable; default every area — narrow it only with a reason, which --lens-area-reason then requires) — the cast is written from these
      --lens-area-reason string      why this run seats fewer than every lens area — REQUIRED with a narrowing --lens-area, refused as empty; recorded in run-config.json so an archived run states what the narrowing was for
      --max-epochs int               the chair sittings the run gets, 1 or more; the sitting that opens the last epoch dispatches nobody, and a run with parties still ready there ends CEILING (default 12; recorded in run-config.json)
      --max-sitting-calls int        the tool calls one seat may make in one sitting; past it the hook refuses every call but a register, and the record says which seat and sitting stopped (default 150; recorded in run-config.json)
      --mint-budget int              the FLOOR of the gaps one lens may hold open at once — a gap that closes frees a mint; each lens's budget is the larger of this and its area's rate over what the area audits, read off the record at each mint — evidence 3 mints for every 2 of the citations on the record; logic 1 mint for every 1 of the claims in the current report; dark-side 3 mints for every 5 of the prose paragraphs in the current report; voice 3 mints for every 5 of the prose paragraphs in the current report; computation 3 mints for every 2 of the proofs on the record; adversary 3 mints for every 5 of the prose paragraphs in the current report; architecture 3 mints for every 5 of the prose paragraphs in the current report (default 15; a smoke run passes 3)
      --model string                 the bulk tier (frontier, blue lanes, red lenses, blue responses)
      --run-id string                the orchestrator's run id, recorded in the .run-live marker so a run found STALE can be resumed rather than only noticed
      --script-path string           the orchestrator script this run was launched from, recorded in the .run-live marker beside --run-id
      --topic string                 the run's research topic (goes in every stub header)
